---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
const baseUrl = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.endsWith("/") ? import.meta.env.BASE_URL : `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Residential Architectural Design GTA | NV Studio Design"
  description="Residential architectural design for custom homes, renovations, and additions in the GTA and Ontario. Zoning, feasibility, permits, and construction drawings."
  keywords="residential architectural design GTA, custom home architect Ontario, home renovation architect Toronto, addition architect GTA, residential permit drawings Toronto"
>
  <section id="one">
    <div class="inner">
      <header class="major">
        <h1>Residential Architectural Design in the GTA &amp; Ontario</h1>
      </header>

      <p>
        NV Studio Design is a residential architecture practice serving
        homeowners and builders across the Greater Toronto Area and Ontario.
        We design custom homes, whole-house renovations, additions, and
        small-scale residential projects &mdash; and we carry each one from
        the first zoning review through permits and construction.
      </p>
      <p>
        Residential work is all we do. That focus is deliberate: houses have
        their own constraints, their own users, and their own municipal
        rules, and a practice that treats them as a sideline rarely gets the
        details right.
      </p>

      <hr />

      <h2>Who this service is for</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Homeowners</strong> planning a new custom home on a GTA lot,
          a major renovation of an older house, or a rear or second-storey
          addition.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Builders and small developers</strong> who need permit-ready
          residential drawings, zoning reviews, or a design partner they can
          hand the site to.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Property owners</strong> exploring what their lot can
          actually support before committing to a direction &mdash; often a
          short feasibility study answers that in a week or two.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What architectural design includes</h2>
      <p>
        We run architectural design as one continuous process from brief to
        handover. A typical project involves most or all of the following:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Site and zoning review.</strong> Checking what the Zoning
          By-law, Official Plan, and any overlays (heritage, ravine, oak
          savannah, laneway) allow on the specific lot.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Feasibility study.</strong> Early massing and floor-plate
          sketches tested against setbacks, height limits, coverage, and FSI
          so the brief meets the rules before we commit to a direction.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Concept design.</strong> Plans, sections, and basic massing
          that resolve the brief into a coherent scheme.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Design development.</strong> Working the scheme through
          materials, structural ideas, envelope details, and interior
          decisions until it&rsquo;s ready for permit.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Permit drawings.</strong> A complete submission package
          sized to what the reviewing municipality actually asks for &mdash;
          not a generic template.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>City coordination.</strong> Responding to Committee of
          Adjustment or plan-examiner comments and getting the file through
          approval.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Consultant coordination.</strong> Working with structural,
          mechanical, and civil engineers, arborists, and surveyors as the
          project needs.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Construction support.</strong> Clarifying drawings,
          answering site questions, and protecting the design decisions as
          the house gets built.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Project types we take on</h2>
      <div class="row gtr-50 gtr-uniform">
        <div class="col-6 col-12-medium">
          <h3>Custom homes</h3>
          <p>
            New residences on GTA and Ontario lots &mdash; infill, teardown
            rebuilds, or greenfield. Designed to the site and the client, not
            pulled from a catalogue.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="col-6 col-12-medium">
          <h3>Whole-house renovations</h3>
          <p>
            Modernising post-war and 1980s houses without pretending they
            were built yesterday. Reworking the plan, the envelope, and the
            interiors as one piece.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="col-6 col-12-medium">
          <h3>Additions</h3>
          <p>
            Rear additions, second storeys, and dormers. Often the hardest
            projects to get right: the new work has to carry its own weight
            and still read as part of the house.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="col-6 col-12-medium">
          <h3>Laneway &amp; secondary suites</h3>
          <p>
            Toronto laneway houses, garden suites, and secondary dwelling
            units across Ontario &mdash; small, tight projects where every
            square foot and every by-law clause matters.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>How we approach the design</h2>
      <p>
        The brief and the site do most of the work. Good residential design
        is usually specific: to a family, to an orientation, to a budget, to
        a by-law. We spend more time understanding those constraints than
        chasing a signature style.
      </p>
      <p>
        In practice that means:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          We test zoning early. No one benefits from a beautiful design
          that can&rsquo;t be built.
        </li>
        <li>
          We design the interior alongside the architecture. Cabinetry,
          lighting, and built-ins are part of the plan, not an afterthought.
        </li>
        <li>
          We draw what the builder will actually use. Clean, legible sets
          that resolve the detail we&rsquo;ve promised on the renders.
        </li>
        <li>
          We prefer fewer, better decisions to long menus of options.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The process, in brief</h2>
      <p>
        Most projects pass through five phases. Each phase has a clear
        deliverable and a clear decision point before we move on. Full
        detail on the <a href={`${baseUrl}process`}>Process page</a>.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>Initial consultation and site review.</li>
        <li>Concept and feasibility &mdash; including the zoning check.</li>
        <li>Design development.</li>
        <li>Permit drawings and municipal approvals.</li>
        <li>Construction coordination.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2>Selected residential work</h2>
      <div class="row gtr-50 gtr-uniform">
        <div class="col-4 col-12-medium">
          <article class="project-card">
            <a href={`${baseUrl}project-modern-residential-villa`} class="image">
              <img
                src="images/projects/modern-residential-villa/1-m5KLpxQggBCWDKl6.jpg"
                alt="Modern residential villa, custom home designed by NV Studio Design in the Greater Toronto Area"
              />
            </a>
            <header class="major">
              <h3><a href={`${baseUrl}project-modern-residential-villa`}>Modern Residential Villa</a></h3>
              <p>Custom home</p>
            </header>
          </article>
        </div>
        <div class="col-4 col-12-medium">
          <article class="project-card">
            <a href={`${baseUrl}project-luxury-home-renovation`} class="image">
              <img
                src="images/projects/luxury-home-renovation/3-A3QOoKVygoIkW0VX.jpg"
                alt="Whole-house renovation of a 1980s residence by NV Studio Design in the GTA"
              />
            </a>
            <header class="major">
              <h3><a href={`${baseUrl}project-luxury-home-renovation`}>Luxury Home Renovation</a></h3>
              <p>Whole-house renovation</p>
            </header>
          </article>
        </div>
        <div class="col-4 col-12-medium">
          <article class="project-card">
            <a href={`${baseUrl}project-laneway-house`} class="image">
              <img
                src="images/projects/laneway-house-project/1a-YD0ng4naVrtpzOMo.jpg"
                alt="Compact 850 square foot laneway house designed by NV Studio Design, Toronto"
              />
            </a>
            <header class="major">
              <h3><a href={`${baseUrl}project-laneway-house`}>Laneway House</a></h3>
              <p>Secondary dwelling</p>
            </header>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
      <ul class="actions">
        <li><a href={`${baseUrl}portfolio`} class="button">View full portfolio</a></li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Working with NV Studio Design</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Residential practice based in the GTA, serving Ontario more broadly.</li>
        <li>Experience with GTA municipal zoning, Committee of Adjustment, and Ontario Building Code requirements.</li>
        <li>
          {"{{ credentials — e.g. OAA licensed architect / BCIN-qualified designer / RAIC — to be filled from questionnaire }}"}
        </li>
        <li>
          {"{{ years_in_practice — to be filled from questionnaire }}"}
        </li>
      </ul>

      <ul class="actions">
        <li><a href={`${baseUrl}contact`} class="button primary">Book a Consultation</a></li>
        <li><a href={`${baseUrl}process`} class="button">See the Process</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
